From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 12:17:25 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] um: wait for idle with one kevent per virtual CPU

An idle virtual CPU on Darwin waited in pselect(), whose read set held
its IPI wake pipe, the host's stop descriptor and a kqueue with its
deadline timer. A wake by the clock cost four calls: a read of the
signal mask, the add of the timer, the wait and the delete of the
timer. CPU0 added a signal and its return, and two dispatches. The timer
is a critical kqueue timer because Darwin coalesces a wait's own
timeout; pselect() stayed for its atomic signal mask.

The wait is now one kevent() on a per-CPU queue. The queue holds the
pipe and the stop descriptor, registered once when the CPU first
idles (a failure panics: a missing registration is a lost wake), and
on CPU0 a user event. The call adds the same one-shot critical timer in
its change list and passes no timeout, so a wake by the clock is that
one call. Nothing disarms the timer. One left by a wake by something
else is replaced by the next wait's add, which also discards a delivery
nobody collected, and a wait with no deadline deletes it in the call
that waits. The wait tracks whether the knote exists, since deleting a
missing one returns an error at once. If the host refuses the timer
the waits fall back to the call's own timeout and warn once.

The result set replaces the fd_set. A pipe that the call reports
carries its byte count: the exit reads those bytes, and one more it
finds, with no poll(), and then takes the pending mask without a second
drain. A call that returns without reporting the pipe found it empty,
as the previous topic has it, and an error drains in full as before.

A wait skips the sleep when the dispatch before it ran a handler, as
it did, since a task the handler made runnable is switched to by the
idle loop and not by the wait.

CPU0's wake from the notification thread is a trigger of the user event
in CPU0's queue, because kevent() has no signal mask to change
atomically and EVFILT_SIGNAL does not report a signal the thread
blocks. The store and load pairs of darwin_irq_idle_begin() and the
parking proof are unchanged; only the transport is, and a trigger sent
after CPU0 left its wait stays pending and ends its next wait early, as
the blocked signal did. SIGUSR2's handler and CPU0's block of it stay.
SIGUSR1 stays blocked on an SME run's carrier throughout, since the
pipe does the waking.

A wake by the clock or the stop descriptor now costs one kevent(),
and a wake by an IPI byte that and one read(). With the signal mask
read, the timer's delete and CPU0's signal return gone, a wake by the
clock takes one call on a secondary CPU, down from four, and three on
CPU0, down from six, which keeps its two dispatches.

